WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:03.759
Film Courage: What is the difference between
a professional level script and one that is

00:00:03.759 --> 00:00:04.990
written by an amateur?

00:00:04.990 --> 00:00:10.240
Mark Sanderson, Screenwriter/Author: A professional
level script I would say can compete in the

00:00:10.240 --> 00:00:16.590
professional marketplace where someone could
receive a screenplay, read it and not go Hmm,

00:00:16.590 --> 00:00:21.911
this has too many problems that this person
is probably not writing at a professional

00:00:21.911 --> 00:00:26.949
level meaning that someone would say Oh my
gosh?

00:00:26.949 --> 00:00:27.949
We could make this!

00:00:27.949 --> 00:00:33.079
That it’s not plagued with the problems
that we spoke of before you know…first time

00:00:33.079 --> 00:00:40.770
overwriting the script and the structure doesn’t
really work and it’s confusing and it’s

00:00:40.770 --> 00:00:45.559
not fulfilling (the ending doesn’t wrap
it up) if you were to read that.

00:00:45.559 --> 00:00:49.380
Also, what we mentioned before format too.

00:00:49.380 --> 00:00:53.610
You look at the script and if it’s all over
the place where it’s kind of clunky and

00:00:53.610 --> 00:01:01.739
weird and it’s not professionally written
(“professionally written”).

00:01:01.739 --> 00:01:10.209
Film Courage: How soon into reading a screenplay
do you know that it’s bad?

00:01:10.209 --> 00:01:16.999
Mark: Well it’s usually in the beginning
but many times you are fooled because the

00:01:16.999 --> 00:01:21.560
beginning is easy to set up so you’re like
Oh this is great and then the longer it goes

00:01:21.560 --> 00:01:28.479
on it runs out of steam and then it just really
is like Okay?

00:01:28.479 --> 00:01:32.009
Or that it is too long.

00:01:32.009 --> 00:01:38.140
It’s like too many pages and there is a
whole other story and a half there that shows

00:01:38.140 --> 00:01:42.340
that it needs to have a lot of editing.

00:01:42.340 --> 00:01:49.280
If it’s a first draft it’s a script and
a half almost (a script and a third let’s

00:01:49.280 --> 00:01:51.719
say) which is overwriting.

00:01:51.719 --> 00:01:57.689
Film Courage: Then a reader will know fairly
quickly?

00:01:57.689 --> 00:02:05.770
Mark: A reader will probably know in the first
page or two if it’s the format giveaway

00:02:05.770 --> 00:02:12.450
or it’s just the style giveaway where you
can tell.

00:02:12.450 --> 00:02:15.140
People all the time post one page on the Internet.

00:02:15.140 --> 00:02:18.610
They put up a page of their script and you
can see it and go Okay?

00:02:18.610 --> 00:02:24.769
It’s a giveaway, it’s a clear giveaway.

00:02:24.769 --> 00:02:27.780
Or you can see another page and go Wow!

00:02:27.780 --> 00:02:29.819
It’s so clear.

00:02:29.819 --> 00:02:33.709
You are seeing what they meant.

00:02:33.709 --> 00:02:36.849
You are seeing what they are writing.

00:02:36.849 --> 00:02:38.260
Not….whoah!

00:02:38.260 --> 00:02:42.860
Bump…who got out of the car?

00:02:42.860 --> 00:02:43.860
Oh?

00:02:43.860 --> 00:02:49.640
And that in a way goes back to format and
how you know what you’re going to put in

00:02:49.640 --> 00:02:51.689
and what you’re not based upon story.

00:02:51.689 --> 00:02:57.170
Film Courage: What do you think about 99 percent
of screenplays being rejected after the first

00:02:57.170 --> 00:02:58.170
page?

00:02:58.170 --> 00:03:02.890
Mark: I don’t know if they are…I think
they would be read.

00:03:02.890 --> 00:03:07.040
They are not thrown in the garbage after page
one.

00:03:07.040 --> 00:03:13.620
I think a script is given a chance, I mean
I would hope from a reader and then it’s

00:03:13.620 --> 00:03:15.790
the overall feeling of it.

00:03:15.790 --> 00:03:20.280
But like we were discussing before if there
is too many bumps and the famous thing is

00:03:20.280 --> 00:03:22.659
Oh this bumps for me.

00:03:22.659 --> 00:03:28.609
It bumps okay it means…I know what they
mean but it probably like “boomp,” you

00:03:28.609 --> 00:03:33.889
know it sort of bumped and not in a good way
where it was confusing or the story was like

00:03:33.889 --> 00:03:37.190
why did it go that way and I didn’t understand
it was this and that.

00:03:37.190 --> 00:03:44.041
A lot of times sadly it’s because of skimming
you know someone just literally I had read

00:03:44.041 --> 00:03:48.459
one time that some people just read the dialogue
or some producer just reads dialogue.

00:03:48.459 --> 00:03:52.530
He doesn’t read the directions, he just
reads the dialogue you know.

00:03:52.530 --> 00:03:53.939
I don’t know?

00:03:53.939 --> 00:04:01.569
That’s not really reading the script but
I think as we were discussing before you can

00:04:01.569 --> 00:04:08.150
tell in the first few pages if it’s going
to be one of those scripts or if it’s going

00:04:08.150 --> 00:04:09.150
to be like Wow!

00:04:09.150 --> 00:04:10.150
This is a page turner.

00:04:10.150 --> 00:04:11.670
I can already see that.

00:04:11.670 --> 00:04:17.639
It’s hard to hide that if you’re not writing
at that particular level.

00:04:17.639 --> 00:04:19.560
So that will be a dead giveaway which is not
good.

00:04:19.560 --> 00:04:23.780
Film Courage: So you think that people give
it more than just a scene?

00:04:23.780 --> 00:04:26.670
Mark: Oh sure, yes.

00:04:26.670 --> 00:04:29.930
The old adage ‘Well if it doesn’t happen
within the first 10 pages.”

00:04:29.930 --> 00:04:32.510
It used to be the first 20 but now it’s
shrunken down.

00:04:32.510 --> 00:04:37.400
If it doesn’t happen…if I am reading and
it doesn’t happen for me, you sort of have

00:04:37.400 --> 00:04:39.900
to know what we are getting into.

00:04:39.900 --> 00:04:45.960
But again these aren’t rules not coming
from me.

00:04:45.960 --> 00:04:55.570
I am just saying that some movies like European
films take longer to happen and.

00:04:55.570 --> 00:05:01.370
I’m talking about Hollywood and if you want
to work in that system, it better happen.

00:05:01.370 --> 00:05:03.759
The reader has got to go “10 pages?

00:05:03.759 --> 00:05:06.110
I still don’t know what is happening?”

00:05:06.110 --> 00:05:08.700
You’ve got to get an idea like Oh we see
this and that.

00:05:08.700 --> 00:05:10.699
This is what they are talking about.

00:05:10.699 --> 00:05:17.990
They will read through the script but if you’re
plagued with typos, three or four page, it

00:05:17.990 --> 00:05:22.199
means you don’t care enough to proofread
your script and you don’t care enough for

00:05:22.199 --> 00:05:26.360
the person who is reading its time so right
away there is a disrespect there.

00:05:26.360 --> 00:05:32.190
Like Well I’ve just got to blow through
it because my idea is so good I don’t have

00:05:32.190 --> 00:05:34.590
to proofread the script.

00:05:34.590 --> 00:05:36.379
Oh okay?

00:05:36.379 --> 00:05:40.659
These are attitudes that won’t serve you
well in the long run.

